  "query_preview": "(Today through Tuesday)...\nIssued at 404 AM EDT Wed Aug 5 2026\n\nToday through Thursday night...\n\nLast portion of recent quieter, yet summery pattern will linger \nthrough this afternoon as modest surface high pressure finally exits \neast.  Broad and stronger early-autumn H500 trough spinning over \nwestern Hudson Bay, and sending solid Canadian air into the Upper \nMidwest...will twist a couple weaker vorts along its southeastern \nperiphery over the Midwest into the late workweek.  This mild \nforcing along with a weaker low-level jet will slowly shift \nnoticeably better moisture just to our northwest...into at least the \nWabash Valley tonight, with perhaps a couple widely scattered \nshowers getting as far as the northwestern two-thirds of the CWA \nThursday.  However, latest trends suggest coverage/intensity of any \nshowers to trend downward Thursday as soon as mid-level support \nslides east of the region.\n\nNevertheless, areas northwest of a Rockville to Kokomo line can \nexpect more scattered shower coverage from late this evening into at \nleast Thursday morning, where isolated storms could have a could \nrobust gusts...with low chances of a few disorganized t-storms far \nsoutheast along the I-69 corridor.  Lack of organized ridging and a \nrather damp column through the latter half of Thursday will still \nfacilitate at least a few showers and a stray TRW.  Another, more \nsubtle wave with associated small bump up of precipitable water \nshould enter the region Thursday night, so kept likely POPs for pre-\ndawn hours Friday, albeit with still lower confidence in any \nappreciable rainfall or stronger cells.\n\nDewpoints today should actually display a narrow minimum axis under \nthe lingering ridge, with mid-60 values possible into early evening \nfrom Washington to Brown County to Muncie. Otherwise light southwest \nwinds will bring more humid and warmer conditions with highs 83-89.\nClouds Thursday to keep readings more moderated with higher humidity \nwith highs mainly low 80s, and mid-80s for far south/southeast zones.\nEarly morning minimums 65-70F through the short term.\n\nFriday Through Saturday.\n\nAn upper-level shortwave trough currently traversing the Upper \nMidwest will drive a surface cold front south-southeastward into the \nOhio Valley by Friday afternoon. Guidance from the latest GFS and \nECMWF along with ensemble clusters shows the main upper-level \ndynamics lifting northeastward into eastern Canada, causing the \nsurface boundary to lose upper support and become quasi-stationary \nacross central Indiana. Deep tropical moisture transport will \nprecede the front, with ensemble mean PWAT values exceeding 1.8 to \n2.0 inches, which sits well above the 90th percentile for early \nAugust climatology.\n\nHREF members indicate modest surface heating beneath cloud cover on \nFriday, yielding MLCAPE values between 1200 and 1800 J/kg by late \nafternoon. Kinematic forcing remains weak, with 0-6 km bulk shear \ngenerally around 15-20 knots, precluding organized severe \nconvection. However, convective initiation along the front and \nlingering mesoscale outflow boundaries will produce multi-cell \nclusters capable of high precipitation efficiency. Given slow storm \nmotion parallel to the surface boundary, localized training of \nstorms and localized flash flooding remain the primary hydrologic \nhazards through Saturday evening before the main moisture axis \nfinally pushes south into Kentucky.\n\nSunday Through Tuesday. \n\nA broader synoptic pattern shift occurs Sunday as an expansive upper-\nlevel ridge over the central Plains expands eastward while a broader \nlongwave trough settles across the northeastern US. This will \nestablish northwesterly flow aloft across central Indiana. With \nbroad ridging to the southwest, rain chances will mostly be limited \nto the central and northern counties through the period with weak \nshortwaves within the broader upper level flow. Daytime highs will \nremain near or slightly above seasonal normals in the mid to upper \n80s, accompanied by very humid dewpoints in the low to mid 70s \ncombining to bring a return to upper 90 to near 100 heat index \nvalues.",
